# New Domain Contracts: Validation, Consumer Registries, and Releases

The domain now describes more than columns and joins. This workbook explores
colocated write policy, computed predicates, dependency declarations, operation
and experience registries, and deterministic consumer releases. It runs without
a database and distinguishes validation from downstream execution support.

## Setup

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install_core!()
```

## 1) Validate before inspecting or projecting

`Selecto.Domain.validate/1` produces a normalized contract and diagnostics.
The inspection/projection helpers can normalize without full validation, so
validate authored input first. `Selecto.configure/3` continues to consume the
authored runtime domain; a consumer projection is a separate artifact.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/action_fixture.exs", __DIR__))
base = SelectoLivebooks.ActionFixture.domain()
assert {:ok, normalized, diagnostics} = Selecto.Domain.validate(base)
assert normalized.writes.fields.state == %{updatable: true}
assert diagnostics.unknown_sections == [:tenant_required]

query_projection = Selecto.Domain.project(normalized, :query)
write_projection = Selecto.Domain.project(normalized, :write)
ui_projection = Selecto.Domain.project(normalized, :ui)
refute Map.has_key?(query_projection, :actions)
assert write_projection.actions.approve.execution.operation == :update
assert ui_projection.actions.approve.selection.eligibility_field == :eligible
```

The fixture's runtime `tenant_required` flag is still classified as an unknown
top-level schema section. Validation retains it for compatibility; an unknown
section diagnostic does not necessarily mean runtime behavior is absent. The
write-side tenant policy is declared separately under `writes.scope.tenant`.
That is the schema 1 shape this fixture uses; section 6 shows schema 2, which
declares tenancy once.

## 2) Declare consumer dependencies and application experiences

`domain_dependencies`, `operations`, `experiences`, and `co_domains` are canonical
schema-v1 sections in this pinned snapshot. The dependency declares which
provider surface is consumed. Its version and fingerprint values are metadata:
the validator does not contact that provider or prove compatibility with it.

`operations` names consumer operation contracts, while `writes.operations`
authorizes relational insert/update/delete behavior. `actions` supplies executable
action templates. `experiences` records host-consumed UI/application definitions.
These similarly named sections serve different purposes.

```elixir
domain = Map.merge(base, %{
  domain_dependencies: [%{
    provider: :carrier_directory, contract: "carrier_lookup_v1", accepts: "~> 1.0",
    uses: %{fields: [:id, :name, :city], filters: [], query_members: []},
    satisfies: [:tenant_id]
  }],
  operations: %{approve: %{version: "1.0.0", description: "Approve a ready item"}},
  experiences: %{"approval-queue" => %{operation: :approve, layout: :table}}
})
assert {:ok, normalized, diagnostics} = Selecto.Domain.validate(domain)
for section <- [:domain_dependencies, :operations, :experiences] do
  assert section in diagnostics.canonical_sections
end
assert {:ok, inspection, _} = Selecto.Domain.describe(normalized)
assert inspection.counts.domain_dependencies == 1
assert inspection.registries.operations == [:approve]
assert inspection.registries.experiences == ["approval-queue"]
inspection.counts
```

The registries validate IDs and map shapes. An experience declaration does not
by itself render a UI, register a route, or guarantee that an operation exists
in a deployed consumer. Those are integration responsibilities.

## 3) Inspect the constrained query contract

This projection exposes named fields and query vocabulary to tooling. The
`internal` flag supports UI behavior; it must not be used as a secrecy boundary.
Redaction, capabilities and host policy need their own explicit design.

```elixir
assert {:ok, query_contract, _} = Selecto.Domain.query_contract(normalized)
assert query_contract.projection == :query_contract
field_ids = Enum.map(query_contract.fields, & &1.id)
assert "id" in field_ids
assert "state" in field_ids
%{fields: field_ids, capabilities: query_contract.capability_ids}
```

## 4) Reject malformed definitions at authoring time

Unknown dependency keys, malformed registries, non-boolean eligibility fields,
and cycles in computed predicates produce diagnostics with codes and paths.

```elixir
malformed = Map.merge(domain, %{
  domain_dependencies: [%{provider: "", contract: nil, surface: :obsolete}],
  experiences: []
})
assert {:error, invalid} = Selecto.Domain.validate(malformed)
codes = Enum.map(invalid.errors, & &1.code)
assert :invalid_domain_dependency_identifier in codes
assert :unknown_domain_dependency_key in codes
assert :invalid_section_shape in codes

bad_eligibility = put_in(base, [:actions, :approve, :selection, :eligibility_field], :state)
assert {:error, invalid_eligibility} = Selecto.Domain.validate(bad_eligibility)
assert Enum.any?(invalid_eligibility.errors, &(&1.code == :invalid_action_eligibility_field))

cyclic = put_in(base, [:source, :columns, :eligible, :computed, :expression], [:eq, :eligible, true])
assert {:error, cycle} = Selecto.Domain.validate(cyclic)
assert Enum.any?(cycle.errors, &(&1.code == :computed_predicate_cycle))

Enum.map(invalid.errors, &Map.take(&1, [:code, :path]))
```

## 5) Build a reproducible consumer release

This release carries operation and experience registries plus the nested
composition contract. Our domain has no nested relationships, so it requires
no nested runtime features. Choose a real runtime/adapter target when checking
a domain with composition; passing a fabricated supported-feature list would
only assert support, not establish it.

```elixir
options = [projection_id: "approval-queue", runtime: "selecto_updato", adapter: "postgresql"]
assert {:ok, release} = Selecto.Domain.consumer_projection_release(domain, options)
assert {:ok, same_release} = Selecto.Domain.consumer_projection_release(domain, Enum.reverse(options))
assert release == same_release
assert release["fingerprint"] =~ "sha256:"
assert release["operations"]["approve"]["version"] == "1.0.0"
assert release["experiences"]["approval-queue"]["operation"] == "approve"
assert [%{"id" => "approve", "version" => "1.0.0"}] = release["dependencies"]["operations"]

changed = put_in(domain, [:operations, :approve, :version], "2.0.0")
assert {:ok, changed_release} = Selecto.Domain.consumer_projection_release(changed, options)
refute release["fingerprint"] == changed_release["fingerprint"]
assert {:error, %{code: :incomplete_consumer_target}} =
  Selecto.Domain.consumer_projection_release(domain, runtime: "selecto_updato")

Map.take(release, ["projection_id", "fingerprint", "required_features", "dependencies"])
```

A changed hash detects changed content; it is not an authorization signature.
The current release compatibility diff concentrates on relationship semantics,
so it must not be treated as a general operation-version compatibility checker.
Inspect the actual registries and the consuming application's contract too.

## 6) Schema 2 documents, the domain fingerprint, and field descriptions

Domain schema 2 (certification specification 3.0) declares tenancy once in a
top-level `tenant`, lists a relation's fields as the keys of `columns` (with an
optional `field_order`), and computes the domain fingerprint: the SHA-256 of
the document's RFC 8785 serialization without `domain_fingerprint`. That
computed value is the domain's identity, so a declared `domain_fingerprint` must
match it. Schema 1 still validates but is deprecated. Only the number 2 is
schema 2; any other document with a top-level `tenant` is refused rather than
loaded without its tenant.

Columns may carry an optional `description` (specification 3.1): 1 to 1000
code points, line feeds allowed, no other control characters.
`Selecto.Domain.field_description/2` reads it from an authored domain of either
schema, or from a normalized one.

```elixir
schema_2 = %{
  "schema_version" => 2,
  "name" => "Approval notes",
  "tenant" => %{"field" => "tenant_id"},
  "source" => %{
    "source_table" => "approval_notes",
    "primary_key" => "id",
    "columns" => %{
      "id" => %{"type" => "integer"},
      "tenant_id" => %{"type" => "integer"},
      "note" => %{"type" => "string", "description" => "Reviewer's note, shown beside the approval."}
    },
    "field_order" => ["id", "tenant_id", "note"],
    "associations" => %{}
  },
  "schemas" => %{},
  "joins" => %{}
}

assert {:ok, normalized_v2, v2_diagnostics} = Selecto.Domain.validate(schema_2)
assert v2_diagnostics.schema_version == 2
fingerprint = Selecto.Domain.SchemaV2.fingerprint(schema_2)
assert normalized_v2.domain_fingerprint == fingerprint
# The validated form is the version 1 contract the rest of Selecto reads.
assert normalized_v2.source["tenant_field"] == "tenant_id"
assert normalized_v2.writes["scope"]["tenant"] == %{"field" => "tenant_id", "required" => true}

assert Selecto.Domain.field_description(schema_2, "note") ==
         "Reviewer's note, shown beside the approval."
assert Selecto.Domain.field_description(schema_2, "id") == nil

error_codes = fn document ->
  {:error, diagnostics} = Selecto.Domain.validate(document)
  Enum.map(diagnostics.errors, & &1.code)
end

wrong_fingerprint = Map.put(schema_2, "domain_fingerprint", String.duplicate("0", 64))
assert :domain_fingerprint_mismatch in error_codes.(wrong_fingerprint)
assert :unknown_domain_key in error_codes.(Map.put(schema_2, "schema_version", "2"))
blank_description = put_in(schema_2, ["source", "columns", "note", "description"], "")
assert :invalid_column_description in error_codes.(blank_description)

%{fingerprint: fingerprint, field_order: normalized_v2.source["fields"]}
```

`Selecto.configure/3` still takes the authored runtime map used throughout these
workbooks; validate a schema 2 document at the contract boundary. A description
is metadata like any other field property: check that a field is public before
showing its description to someone.

Try [action planning](selecto_actions_workbook.livemd) for typed variants and
capability decisions, then [co-domain lookups](selecto_co_domains_workbook.livemd)
for executable search scope and computed eligibility.
